## Why this matters

The capstone extends the reference architecture with the boundary that server-rendered component trees introduce: what may cross from server to client as props, where mutations run, how the client shows optimistic results, and how a bad decision is reversed. Frameworks differ in what their serialization protocol accepts and how they expose server-side mutations; the lab therefore builds a framework-independent boundary check and records the framework's rules as of the version you test, rather than assuming them.

## The simple version

Think of the server and client trees as two offices connected by a pneumatic tube. Only things that fit the tube travel: plain data, references to server-side functions, and a few agreed container types. A chair does not fit, however useful it is in the other office. Mutations are letters sent back through the tube: they must say who sent them, be validated on arrival, and the sender may pin a provisional copy on the wall until the reply comes. The analogy breaks at identity: a data object sent through the tube is a copy, so the client cannot mutate it and expect the server to notice; it must send the letter.

## The lab

Run the setup in an isolated directory. It creates a small Node project with a boundary checker, a mutation contract and a test; it touches nothing outside the temporary directory and needs no framework installed.

```bash
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" || exit 1
mkdir -p src test
cat > src/boundary.mjs <<'JS'
// Props that may cross a serialization boundary. Adjust ALLOWED to your framework's documented rules and record the version.
const ALLOWED = new Set(['string', 'number', 'boolean', 'undefined']);
export function firstViolation(value, path = 'props') {
  if (value === null || ALLOWED.has(typeof value)) return null;
  if (typeof value === 'function') return value.$$serverAction ? null : `${path}: functions cannot cross unless they are server actions`;
  if (Array.isArray(value)) { for (const [i, item] of value.entries()) { const v = firstViolation(item, `${path}[${i}]`); if (v) return v; } return null; }
  if (value instanceof Date) return null;
  if (typeof value === 'object') {
    if (Object.getPrototypeOf(value) !== Object.prototype && !(value instanceof Map) && !(value instanceof Set)) return `${path}: class instances do not cross the boundary`;
    for (const [key, item] of Object.entries(value)) { const v = firstViolation(item, `${path}.${key}`); if (v) return v; }
    return null;
  }
  return `${path}: unsupported ${typeof value}`;
}
JS
printf 'lab at %s\n' "$lab"
```

## Core mechanics

The boundary contract is a list, checked by code, of what server components may pass to client components: serializable data by structure, server action references, and the container types your framework documents. `firstViolation` returns the first offending path so a failing build names the prop. The mutation contract defines each server action's input (validated on the server, never trusted from the client), its result shape (success with the new record or failure with a reason), and its invalidation effect (which cached keys change). The client applies an optimistic copy tagged as pending, replaces it with the server result, and rolls back with the failure reason. Because the client holds copies, every mutation goes through an action; there is no path where editing a prop changes server state.

## Runnable experiment

Predict each printed line, then run `node` on this script in the lab.

```js
import { firstViolation } from './src/boundary.mjs';
const action = Object.assign(async () => {}, { $$serverAction: true });
console.log(firstViolation({ id: 1, title: 'x', tags: ['a'], updatedAt: new Date() }));   // null
console.log(firstViolation({ onClick: () => {} }));              // props.onClick: functions cannot cross unless they are server actions
console.log(firstViolation({ save: action }));                   // null
class Money { constructor(c) { this.cents = c; } }
console.log(firstViolation({ price: new Money(100) }));          // props.price: class instances do not cross the boundary
```

Then write `test/mutation.test.mjs` with the Node test runner: an optimistic update followed by a failed action must leave the list equal to its state before the update and expose the failure reason.

## Failure modes

Passing a callback from a server component "because it works in development" fails at the boundary in production or silently drops behavior. Validating input on the client only lets any caller of the action bypass the rules. Optimistic updates without a rollback leave phantom records after a failure. Treating a prop object as mutable state on the client creates edits that never reach the server. Relying on undocumented serialization support for a type ties the architecture to one framework version.

## Practical exercise

Deliver the reference-architecture extension: `ADR.md` (the decision to place mutations behind validated server actions with optimistic client copies, the alternatives considered, and the signal that would reverse it), `BOUNDARY.md` (the allowed-props list with the framework and version it was checked against, and the checker's output for three real component props), the mutation contract for two actions with their validation and invalidation effects, the passing test for optimistic rollback, and a rollback path that moves one mutation back to a conventional request handler without changing the client contract.

exercise.starter_code:
// test/mutation.test.mjs (sketch)
import test from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
test('a failed action rolls the optimistic copy back', async () => {
  let items = [{ id: 1, title: 'a' }];
  const before = structuredClone(items);
  const pending = { id: 'tmp', title: 'b', pending: true };
  items = [...items, pending];
  const result = { ok: false, reason: 'title taken' };
  if (!result.ok) items = items.filter((i) => i !== pending);
  assert.deepEqual(items, before);
  assert.equal(result.reason, 'title taken');
});
